> Then what is the big difference between NetBSD and OpenBSD :)

Oh, you mean the _big_ difference.

The NetBSD project has the conscious design that they intend to run
their OS on virtually whatever kind of hardware anyone imagines
running it on.  That probably doesn't include AS/400 or Z-80 machines,
but it's a close thing.

The FreeBSD project has as its intent to build a stable 80386-based
version of BSD.  It has since expanded to newer Intel and AMD
processors, and some people have added on support for some other
architectures including SPARC and Alpha.  The "multi-architecture
thing" is very much a secondary factor in the project.

It is likely to be a better idea to try running NetBSD on unusual
hardware like Alpha; that's more up its alley...
