First thing I want to say is, I have been using Redhat running Apache on an
old X86 box for years and have been very happy with it.  Recently I decided
to strip NT4.0 from my old Axpci133 box and switch it to Redhat Linux.  I
first discovered that 7.2 was not available for the Alpha from Redhat, so I
downloaded the 7.1 iso images and burned the disks.  Then when trying to
boot off the CD I was finally convinced that the ARC bios had to do so I
flashed the rom to use SRM.  After setting up SRM and getting it to work it
still wouldn't boot from the CD, I kept getting a compression error of the
compressed vmlinux.gz on the CD.
So I created boot floppies and tried again.  This time I was able to read
the CD and start the install.  Since I was using SRM (recommended by RedHat)
I had to use only devices recognized by SRM to boot with.  This meant that
the small 500meg scsi drive that was attached to the integrated SCSI port
(CD is attached there too.) had to serve as the spot for my aboot loader.
Redhat would see my 20 and 40 gig IDE drives, but would not allow me to use
them as an install point for /root (from what I've been able to see
apparently the aboot block must be on the same device as /root.)
After I finally got it installed  (I had to use text mode as X failed to
start, this should have been a warning for me.)  There was no way in he!!
that I could get X to run.  I was using a  Compaq packaged Matrox Impression
2 meg PCI video card.  It wouldn't run under any of the VGA or SVGA drivers.
So I tried an ATI Rage Pro Turbo PCI video card, well the Axpci133 wouldn't
even operate with the card, I got no video output at all.  With both the
Matrox and ATI video cards, the SRM show device command would show both
cards as video PCI cards, but the ATI just wouldn't work.  So I dug into my
junk box and pulled out an ATI Graphics Pro Turbo Mach64 board ISA board.
Card works but still no X, using the XF86_Mach64 driver.  No X under VGA,
SVGA, or VGA mono drivers either.  No matter which monitor selection I tried
or what sync rates I tried I would still get the message that no screens
were found for any of the resolutions.  So then I tried an even older ATI
Mach32 video card, same problem, then an old S-3 based PCI, same problem
again.
Then I decided to try FreeBSD Alpha install, it would boot from the CD but
about 1/2 way through startup it would blow up with a kernel panic message
and die.
Then I tried Debian for Alpha, same results as the Redhat, except it would
not even see my IDE HD's,  so I go back to Redhat and start over.  I have
tried 6 different PCI and ISA video cards and none of them will work under
X.
I give up, I'm going to junk the Axpci133 and go back to X86 based computers
from now on unless anyone here has any suggestions.






